        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-20T21:59:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why do we poll device state every second, but also interrupt  and call this function? Is that just to get better granularity of events? It seems to me like we should be interrupting on low-\u003ehigh and high-\u003elow transitions. I\u0027ll look through the rest of the stack, but our approach seems odd to me.\n\nAlso, I understand what you are doing here in this patch is orthogonal to almost everything I said.",
      "revId": "d0407f17804f5c707db0578b2293a55cb72f26d3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c865c6f6_ee6397c0",
        "filename": "board/cr50/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 753,
      "author": {
        "id": 1136059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-20T22:11:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We can\u0027t keep the high-\u003elow or low-\u003ehigh interrupts enabled all the time because there are just so many transitions on the UART signals.\n\nWe are mostly just polling. The device_state_on interrupt is used to get granularity. It is disabled whenever we think that the device is on and is pulling the signal up. When the device is off, we leave the low-\u003ehigh interrupt enabled so we can detect immediately if the device turns on.",
      "parentUuid": "c865c6f6_0e170b67",
      "revId": "d0407f17804f5c707db0578b2293a55cb72f26d3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c865c6f6_621d6adc",
        "filename": "board/cr50/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 753,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-23T21:59:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As you noted that\u0027s for the UART case. But on x86 we shouldn\u0027t see high frequency interrupts on a signal that is generally stable. I feel like we should remove GPIO_DETECT_AP from this device_state thing entirely. Something for a different CL, but we seem to be wedging things into previous assumptions that are no longer true.",
      "parentUuid": "c865c6f6_ee6397c0",
      "revId": "d0407f17804f5c707db0578b2293a55cb72f26d3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
